From d78f7e49c821df29ab12b673be11135980ac4363 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: stephan-xyz <stephan@zehrer.net>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 19:10:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] bcm2835-hdmiaudiod: HDMI sound of the Raspberry Pi 3B+ and
 Zero 2 W

A driver for the audio part of the VC4 HDMI block, as Linux vc4_hdmi.c
programs it, offering the audiohw scheme for audiod in place of
bcm2835-pwmaudiod (issue #423 in redox-rpi). The firmware keeps the
picture; no display driver is needed.

- MAI FIFO (HD block 0x7e808000): reset, sample clock MAI_SMP from the
  HSM clock, 2 channels PCM 44.1 kHz, thresholds as Linux.
- HDMI core (0x7e902000): channel map and mask, audio packet set-up
  (B-frame identifier 8), audio clock regeneration (N = 128 fs / 1000,
  CTS from the pixel clock: rpi-firmwared, else PLLH_PIX from the
  clock manager, else 148.5 MHz), the audio info frame
  in packet RAM slot 4. DVI mode is switched to HDMI mode.
- DMA channel 0 with DREQ 17 plays a ring of 8 x 512 frames of IEC958
  subframes (preamble, channel status, parity) into MAI_DATA.
- The HSM rate comes from CM_HSMCTL/HSMDIV if the source is known,
  else 163.682864 MHz. Two 3 s measurements of the DMA progress that
  agree correct it once; a refresh gap over 60 ms (a ring turn is 93 ms,
  turns would go uncounted) starts the measurement over.
- A 10 ms timer counts FIFO underflows and overflows and programs the
  audio part again if the firmware reset it.
- stats, tone (a sine through the ring) and stop as in
  bcm2835-pwmaudiod. Without an HDMI output (QEMU) the ring plays to
  the clock.

16 host tests: IEC958 subframes, channel status, block alignment
across slots, register values, N/CTS, MAI_SMP, info frame and its
packet RAM layout.

The service replaces bcm2835-pwmaudiod in 40_drivers.target.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
